{"description":"Wire and harden steward's credential source so it works under the plugin host's minimal-environment whitelist (PATH, HOME, EKA_PLUGIN_DIR, SystemRoot). The Resolver seam and a file-backed resolver already exist (internal/secrets/secrets.go Resolver/Broker; internal/secrets/file.go NewFileResolver); the true delta is: connect NewFileResolver to the broker as the production credential source; harden file.go (open with O_NOFOLLOW, require a regular file owned by the current uid, require mode 0600 with no group/other bits, cap the file size, refuse every violation deterministically); and add EKA_HOME to the plugin environment whitelist in both eka-core/plugin and eka-cli/cmd (a non-secret ecosystem coordinate), ideally via one shared helper to avoid the two whitelist copies drifting. The worker model is unchanged: workers receive only the driver-declared credential names, resolved by the broker, over a base environment carrying no HOME, no EKA_HOME, no PATH and no credential variable. Document the trust boundary honestly: the whitelist prevents credential inheritance via the environment, not filesystem access, so a same-uid process can read a user-owned file.","acceptanceCriteria":"steward resolves a declared credential from the hardened file-backed resolver. | The resolver refuses (deterministically) a symlink, a non-regular file, a file not owned by the current uid, a file with any group/other permission bit, and an over-size file. | EKA_HOME is added to the plugin environment whitelist consistently in eka-cli and eka-core. | No credential is ever exposed to a worker beyond its driver's declaration; the base worker environment is unchanged. | A missing required credential remains a deterministic error naming the tool and the variable. | The trust boundary (the OS user, not the plugin) is documented in docs/secrets.md."}